\[
R \;=\; \frac{\text{Rep}_{\text{GT}}}{\text{Rep}_{\text{MO}}}
\]

- \(\text{Rep}_{\text{GT}}\) = number of repetitions when the model is asked to continue **from the ground‑truth prefix**.  
- \(\text{Rep}_{\text{MO}}\) = number of repetitions when the model continues **from its own generated prefix**.

Because the overall objective is **to minimise repetition**, a **smaller** value of \(R\) is preferable.  

| Value of \(R\) | Interpretation |
|----------------|----------------|
| \(R < 1\)      | Fewer repeats under the ground‑truth seed → good. |
| \(R = 1\)      | No difference between the two seeds. |
| \(R > 1\)      | More repeats under the ground‑truth seed than under the model’s own seed → the model is *amplifying* repetition. |

Hence **a larger ratio is worse**; it signals that forcing the model to start from the external (ground‑truth) prefix makes it repeat more often, which goes against the goal of keeping repetitions low.

